1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:01,986
The Science Behind Cannabis.

2
00:00:01,986 --> 00:00:04,381
Episode one: How cannabis research began.

3
00:00:04,381 --> 00:00:10,691
In this episode we follow the story of how chemists and pharmacologists worked out what cannabis is made of,

4
00:00:10,691 --> 00:00:16,125
and how that work led to the discovery of a signalling system that exists in every one of us.

5
00:00:17,625 --> 00:00:22,248
Cannabis sativa has been cultivated for thousands of years, for fibre, seed and oil,

6
00:00:22,248 --> 00:00:26,266
and as a preparation in traditional medicine in Asia and the Middle East.

7
00:00:26,266 --> 00:00:30,449
In the 1840s the Irish physician William O'Shaughnessy, working in Calcutta,

8
00:00:30,449 --> 00:00:33,366
described cannabis preparations to European medicine.

9
00:00:33,366 --> 00:00:37,384
For the rest of the nineteenth century, scientists could study the plant,

10
00:00:37,384 --> 00:00:41,898
but they could not say which molecules inside it were responsible for its effects.

11
00:00:41,898 --> 00:00:45,035
That question would take about a hundred years to answer.

12
00:00:46,535 --> 00:00:51,215
The first cannabis constituent, cannabinol, was obtained in the 1890s by Wood,

13
00:00:51,215 --> 00:00:55,415
Spivey and Easterfield in Cambridge, from a resin they called red oil.

14
00:00:55,415 --> 00:00:57,695
But progress then stalled for decades.

15
00:00:57,695 --> 00:01:02,435
Cannabis constituents are oily, fat-loving molecules that do not form crystals,

16
00:01:02,435 --> 00:01:09,395
and in the early twentieth century chemists had no chromatography and no spectroscopy to separate and identify them.

17
00:01:09,395 --> 00:01:17,735
In the 1940s Roger Adams in the United States and Alexander Todd in Britain isolated cannabidiol and clarified the structure of cannabinol,

18
00:01:17,735 --> 00:01:21,996
but the exact structures of the main constituents were still uncertain.

19
00:01:23,496 --> 00:01:25,516
The breakthrough came in Israel.

20
00:01:25,516 --> 00:01:31,072
In 1963, Raphael Mechoulam and Yuval Shvo established the full structure of cannabidiol.

21
00:01:31,072 --> 00:01:37,638
One year later, in 1964, Yehiel Gaoni and Mechoulam isolated delta-9-tetrahydrocannabinol, known as THC,

22
00:01:37,638 --> 00:01:40,289
in pure form and determined its structure.

23
00:01:40,289 --> 00:01:45,087
They showed that THC is the principal psychoactive constituent of the plant.

24
00:01:45,087 --> 00:01:50,706
Over the following years, the group also completed the total synthesis of both molecules,

25
00:01:50,706 --> 00:01:55,126
so that pure, well-defined compounds were finally available for study.

26
00:01:56,626 --> 00:01:58,707
Having pure THC changed everything.

27
00:01:58,707 --> 00:01:59,837
For the first time,

28
00:01:59,837 --> 00:02:05,368
pharmacologists could give animals a known dose of a single molecule and measure what it did.

29
00:02:05,368 --> 00:02:10,244
They could also make chemical variants, change one part of the molecule at a time,

30
00:02:10,244 --> 00:02:12,088
and see which changes mattered.

31
00:02:12,088 --> 00:02:18,451
This structure–activity work, carried out in the 1970s and eighties by groups including those of Mechoulam,

32
00:02:18,451 --> 00:02:21,722
Roger Pertwee and Allyn Howlett, raised a new question:

33
00:02:21,722 --> 00:02:25,409
how does a fat-loving molecule like THC actually act on cells?

34
00:02:25,409 --> 00:02:28,561
That question leads directly to the next discoveries.

35
00:02:30,061 --> 00:02:35,148
Between 1988 and 2001, the field moved from plant chemistry to biology.

36
00:02:35,148 --> 00:02:42,385
In 1988, Allyn Howlett's laboratory identified a specific binding site for cannabinoids in rat brain.

37
00:02:42,385 --> 00:02:45,896
In 1990, the receptor, called CB one, was cloned.

38
00:02:45,896 --> 00:02:54,065
In 1992, Mechoulam's group, with William Devane and Lumír Hanuš, discovered anandamide, the first endocannabinoid,

39
00:02:54,065 --> 00:02:56,573
a molecule made by the body itself.

40
00:02:56,573 --> 00:03:02,448
A second receptor, CB two, was cloned in 1993, and a second endocannabinoid, 2-AG,

41
00:03:02,448 --> 00:03:03,738
was found in 1995.

42
00:03:03,738 --> 00:03:09,542
By 2001, researchers had shown how these molecules carry signals between neurons.

43
00:03:11,042 --> 00:03:14,887
The history tells us something important about how cannabis works.

44
00:03:14,887 --> 00:03:18,091
Its constituents do not act in some vague, general way.

45
00:03:18,091 --> 00:03:23,801
They act on a specific biological system, the endocannabinoid system, which consists of receptors,

46
00:03:23,801 --> 00:03:29,277
the body's own signalling molecules, and the enzymes that make and break those molecules down.

47
00:03:29,277 --> 00:03:33,646
This system is present in all vertebrates and is part of normal physiology.

48
00:03:33,646 --> 00:03:36,093
The plant molecules happen to fit into it.

49
00:03:36,093 --> 00:03:40,230
In the rest of this series we look at each part of that system in turn.

50
00:03:41,730 --> 00:03:45,847
In the next episode, we look at the molecules themselves: what cannabinoids are,

51
00:03:45,847 --> 00:03:48,523
how they are built, and how the plant produces them.
